  "mayorWinnerName": "Ken Livingstone",
  "turnout": 33.7,
  "summary": "The first elections for the new Greater London Authority created the office of Mayor of London and a 25-member London Assembly. Ken Livingstone, who had been refused the Labour nomination, won as an independent with 667,877 first-preference votes (39.0%), defeating Conservative Steve Norris and pushing the official Labour candidate, Frank Dobson, into third. Livingstone was elected with 776,427 votes (57.9%) after second preferences. Labour and the Conservatives tied on nine Assembly seats each, with the Liberal Democrats on four and the Greens on three.",
  "highlights": [
    "First-ever election for the Mayor of London and the London Assembly",
    "Ken Livingstone wins as an independent after being denied Labour's nomination",
    "Livingstone elected with 57.9% after second preferences",
    "Labour and the Conservatives tie on nine Assembly seats each",
    "Greens enter the Assembly with three list seats on 11.1% of the vote"
  ],
